reopened in 1979. He points up to the very top of the building's roof. There are cooling towers, which would operate to eject heat from from the hotel. Fraser explains that Legionella bacteria occurs naturally in the environment, which can make it hard to pin down a specific source. But it really likes to grow in warm water, and one ideal environment is the warm water basins in air conditioning systems. When those systems convert warm water into cold air, they produce a mist as a by-product. And that mist can come across the edge of the roof and layer down the side of a building.
